/*
 * ============================================================================
 * Zamani Universal Programming Language
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * File:
 *     grammar/effects/code_generation.g4
 *
 * Grammar identity:
 *     CodeGenerationEffects
 *
 * Status:
 *     Production modular effect-domain adapter
 *
 * Grammar technology:
 *     ANTLR4 parser grammar
 *
 * Language/compiler baseline:
 *     Rust 1.97 / Rust 1.97.1
 *     Rust Edition 2021
 *
 * Safety:
 *     No embedded Rust.
 *     No unsafe code.
 *     No semantic predicates.
 *     No parser actions.
 *     No runtime execution.
 *     No filesystem access.
 *     No network access.
 *     No environment inspection.
 *     No hardware discovery.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 1. PURPOSE
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * This file defines the CODE-GENERATION EFFECT boundary of Zamani.
 *
 * It is deliberately an adapter rather than a second code-generation
 * language.
 *
 * The distinction is:
 *
 *     grammar/metaprogramming/generation.g4
 *         |
 *         +--> owns generation SOURCE SYNTAX
 *
 *     grammar/effects/effect-operations.g4
 *         |
 *         +--> owns generic EFFECT OPERATION USE
 *
 *     grammar/effects/effect-sets.g4
 *         |
 *         +--> owns EFFECT REFERENCES / EFFECT SETS
 *
 *     this file
 *         |
 *         +--> owns the CODE-GENERATION EFFECT DOMAIN BOUNDARY
 *
 * Code generation is therefore treated as an effect-domain classification
 * over canonical effect syntax.
 *
 * This file MUST NOT become a second implementation of:
 *
 *     generation
 *     generationRequest
 *     generationTargetedRequest
 *     macro expansion
 *     quotation
 *     reflection
 *     specialization
 *     compile-time execution
 *     syntax-tree construction
 *     source emission
 *
 * Those facilities have their own authoritative owners.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 2. ARCHITECTURAL AUTHORITY
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Repository architecture:
 *
 *     grammar/DESIGN.md
 *
 * Grammar entry point:
 *
 *     grammar/README.md
 *
 * Canonical parser composition:
 *
 *     grammar/Zamani.g4
 *     grammar/antlr/ZamaniParser.g4
 *
 * Canonical lexer:
 *
 *     grammar/antlr/ZamaniLexer.g4
 *
 * Canonical effect composition:
 *
 *     grammar/effects/effects.g4
 *
 * Generic effect operation syntax:
 *
 *     grammar/effects/effect-operations.g4
 *
 * Generic effect-set syntax:
 *
 *     grammar/effects/effect-sets.g4
 *
 * Code-generation source syntax:
 *
 *     grammar/metaprogramming/generation.g4
 *
 * Metaprogramming composition:
 *
 *     grammar/metaprogramming/metaprogramming.g4
 *
 * Semantic/contract specifications:
 *
 *     grammar/spec/
 *     grammar/specification/
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 3. OWNERSHIP
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * THIS FILE OWNS:
 *
 *     codeGenerationEffect
 *     codeGenerationEffectReference
 *     codeGenerationEffectReferenceList
 *     codeGenerationEffectSet
 *     codeGenerationEffectOperationReference
 *     codeGenerationEffectInvocation
 *     codeGenerationEffectOperationUse
 *
 * These are domain adapters around canonical generic effect constructs.
 *
 * THIS FILE DOES NOT OWN:
 *
 *     identifier
 *     qualifiedName
 *     expression
 *     typeExpression
 *     argumentList
 *     attributes
 *     effectDeclaration
 *     effectOperationDeclaration
 *     effectReference
 *     effectReferenceList
 *     effectSet
 *     effectOperationReference
 *     effectInvocation
 *     effectOperationUse
 *     generation
 *     generationRequest
 *     generationTargetedRequest
 *     macro syntax
 *     reflection syntax
 *     specialization syntax
 *     quotation syntax
 *     compile-time execution syntax
 *     capabilities
 *     resources
 *     contracts
 *     policies
 *     provenance
 *     security
 *     canonical AST implementation
 *     semantic analysis
 *     canonical IR
 *     quantum::ir
 *     HDL representation
 *     hardware realization
 *     runtime dispatch
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 4. DEPENDENCY CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * DEPENDS_ON:
 *
 *     Core
 *     Expressions
 *     EffectOperations
 *     EffectSets
 *
 * EXPORTS:
 *
 *     codeGenerationEffect
 *     codeGenerationEffectReference
 *     codeGenerationEffectReferenceList
 *     codeGenerationEffectSet
 *     codeGenerationEffectOperationReference
 *     codeGenerationEffectInvocation
 *     codeGenerationEffectOperationUse
 *
 * CONSUMED_BY:
 *
 *     code-generation semantic analysis
 *     effect analysis
 *     metaprogramming semantic analysis
 *     compiler conformance tooling
 *     effect-domain validation
 *     grammar/effects consumers that explicitly need code-generation
 *         classification
 *
 * AST_OWNER:
 *
 *     domain-neutral frontend AST
 *
 * SEMANTIC_OWNER:
 *
 *     effect semantic analysis
 *     metaprogramming/code-generation semantic analysis
 *
 * IR_OWNER:
 *
 *     canonical semantic representation
 *     downstream classical / quantum / HDL / hardware / distributed /
 *     accelerator representations as appropriate
 *
 * TEST_OWNER:
 *
 *     grammar/tests/effects/code_generation/
 *
 * SPEC_OWNER:
 *
 *     grammar/spec/effects.md
 *     grammar/specification/
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 5. LEXER CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * The canonical lexer vocabulary is consumed.
 *
 * This file MUST NOT define lexer rules.
 *
 * This file MUST NOT introduce:
 *
 *     CODEGEN
 *     GENERATE
 *     EMIT
 *     TEMPLATE
 *     SOURCE
 *     TARGET
 *     BACKEND
 *     LANGUAGE
 *
 * as local tokens.
 *
 * Code-generation effect identity is open-world.
 *
 * Existing lexical spellings, including any legacy code-generation keyword
 * already recognized by the generic effect grammar, remain governed by the
 * canonical lexer.
 *
 * This adapter does not require a new keyword merely to classify an effect.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 6. GRAMMAR DEPENDENCIES
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Core provides canonical:
 *
 *     qualifiedName
 *     identifier
 *     names
 *     attributes
 *     other language-wide syntax
 *
 * Expressions provides canonical:
 *
 *     expression
 *     argumentList
 *     other expression-level syntax
 *
 * EffectOperations provides:
 *
 *     effectOperationReference
 *     effectInvocation
 *     effectOperationUse
 *
 * EffectSets provides:
 *
 *     effectReference
 *     effectReferenceList
 *     effectSet
 *
 * This file MUST NOT redefine any of those constructs.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 7. EFFECT / GENERATION SEPARATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Code generation has two distinct language concerns.
 *
 * SOURCE GENERATION SYNTAX:
 *
 *     generation
 *     generationRequest
 *     generationTargetedRequest
 *     generationInput
 *     generation options
 *
 * These belong to:
 *
 *     grammar/metaprogramming/generation.g4
 *
 * EFFECT REPRESENTATION:
 *
 *     an operation that semantically performs or requests code generation
 *
 * This file provides the second boundary.
 *
 * Therefore the following conceptual relationship is intentional:
 *
 *     generation source construct
 *             |
 *             v
 *     semantic generation operation
 *             |
 *             v
 *     code-generation effect
 *
 * and:
 *
 *     code-generation effect
 *             |
 *             v
 *     generic effect system
 *
 * This avoids coupling the generic effect system to the complete
 * metaprogramming grammar.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 8. OPEN-WORLD CODE-GENERATION EFFECT MODEL
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Code-generation effect identities are OPEN-WORLD.
 *
 * The grammar MUST NOT enumerate:
 *
 *     source languages
 *     target languages
 *     output formats
 *     compiler backends
 *     generators
 *     template engines
 *     code emitters
 *     architectures
 *     processors
 *     accelerators
 *     vendors
 *     operating systems
 *     file formats
 *     intermediate representations
 *     device types
 *
 * Examples of possible semantic identities include:
 *
 *     code_generation::generate
 *     code_generation::emit
 *     code_generation::transform
 *     code_generation::specialize
 *     code_generation::derive
 *     code_generation::materialize
 *     generator::source
 *     generator::artifact
 *     compiler::generation
 *     dialect::generation
 *     vendor::extension::generation
 *     future::code_generation::operation
 *
 * These are examples of possible semantic identities only.
 *
 * They are NOT a built-in vocabulary.
 *
 * Whether a name represents a code-generation effect is determined by
 * semantic resolution and the registered semantic environment.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 9. WHY OPERATION NAMES ARE NOT ENUMERATED
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Zamani must remain extensible.
 *
 * A future generator may generate:
 *
 *     Zamani source
 *     another language
 *     HDL
 *     quantum source
 *     accelerator source
 *     configuration
 *     data representation
 *     deployment description
 *     executable representation
 *     hardware description
 *     future computational representation
 *
 * The universal parser must not require a grammar modification for each new
 * generation mechanism.
 *
 * Therefore:
 *
 *     operation identity = source-level name
 *
 * while:
 *
 *     operation meaning = semantic information
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 10. PUBLIC ENTRY POINT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * `codeGenerationEffect` is the principal public entry point owned by this
 * grammar.
 *
 * It deliberately delegates all ordinary effect syntax.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 */

parser grammar CodeGenerationEffects;

options {
    tokenVocab = ZamaniLexer;
}

import Core,
       Expressions,
       EffectOperations,
       EffectSets;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 11. CODE-GENERATION EFFECT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * This is the principal domain adapter.
 *
 * It provides reusable parser boundaries for consumers that need to
 * explicitly identify code-generation-related effect syntax.
 *
 * It does not prove semantic code-generation classification.
 */

codeGenerationEffect
    : codeGenerationEffectReference
    | codeGenerationEffectReferenceList
    | codeGenerationEffectSet
    | codeGenerationEffectInvocation
    | codeGenerationEffectOperationUse
    | codeGenerationEffectOperationReference
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 12. CODE-GENERATION EFFECT REFERENCE
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Delegates effect identity to the canonical effect-reference grammar.
 *
 * Examples of possible semantic identities:
 *
 *     code_generation::generate
 *     code_generation::emit
 *     compiler::generation
 *     future::generation::operation
 *
 * These examples do not establish built-in operations.
 */

codeGenerationEffectReference
    : effectReference
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 13. CODE-GENERATION EFFECT REFERENCE LIST
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Delegates collection syntax to the canonical effect-set subsystem.
 *
 * Source order is preserved by the parse structure.
 *
 * Semantic normalization occurs downstream.
 */

codeGenerationEffectReferenceList
    : effectReferenceList
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 14. CODE-GENERATION EFFECT SET
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Delegates effect-set syntax to the generic effect-set owner.
 *
 * A set may contain code-generation effects alongside any other effects.
 *
 * Conceptual example:
 *
 *     {
 *         code_generation::generate,
 *         io::read,
 *         provenance::record
 *     }
 *
 * This file does not classify every member.
 *
 * Semantic analysis performs domain classification.
 */

codeGenerationEffectSet
    : effectSet
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 15. CODE-GENERATION EFFECT OPERATION REFERENCE
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Delegates operation identity to EffectOperations.
 *
 * No generator catalogue is embedded here.
 */

codeGenerationEffectOperationReference
    : effectOperationReference
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 16. CODE-GENERATION EFFECT INVOCATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Delegates operation invocation to the generic effect-operation subsystem.
 *
 * Conceptual examples:
 *
 *     code_generation::generate(source)
 *     code_generation::emit(ast)
 *     code_generation::specialize(program, parameters)
 *
 * These examples describe source shape only.
 *
 * They do not establish built-in operations.
 */

codeGenerationEffectInvocation
    : effectInvocation
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 17. CODE-GENERATION EFFECT OPERATION USE
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Delegates effect-operation use to the canonical effect-operation grammar.
 *
 * This is the principal bridge for:
 *
 *     perform code_generation::generate(...)
 *
 * without redefining `perform`.
 */

codeGenerationEffectOperationUse
    : effectOperationUse
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 18. INTEGRATION ALIASES
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * These aliases provide stable named boundaries for tooling and semantic
 * consumers without introducing additional syntax.
 */

/*
 * Reference-only integration boundary.
 */
codeGenerationEffectReferenceConstruct
    : codeGenerationEffectReference
    ;


/*
 * Operation-reference-only integration boundary.
 */
codeGenerationEffectOperationReferenceConstruct
    : codeGenerationEffectOperationReference
    ;


/*
 * Invocation-only integration boundary.
 */
codeGenerationEffectInvocationConstruct
    : codeGenerationEffectInvocation
    ;


/*
 * Operation-use integration boundary.
 */
codeGenerationEffectOperationUseConstruct
    : codeGenerationEffectOperationUse
    ;


/*
 * Effect-set integration boundary.
 */
codeGenerationEffectSetConstruct
    : codeGenerationEffectSet
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 19. GENERATION-SYNTAX BOUNDARY
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * The generation grammar owns generation source syntax.
 *
 * This file MUST NOT import:
 *
 *     grammar/metaprogramming/generation.g4
 *
 * merely to classify generation effects.
 *
 * That would unnecessarily couple:
 *
 *     effect infrastructure
 *
 * to:
 *
 *     metaprogramming syntax
 *
 * and could create an import cycle.
 *
 * The semantic layer joins:
 *
 *     generation construct
 *
 * with:
 *
 *     code-generation effect
 *
 * after parsing.
 *
 * The intended architecture is:
 *
 *     source
 *       |
 *       +-----------------------------+
 *       |                             |
 *       v                             v
 * generation syntax            effect syntax
 *       |                             |
 *       v                             v
 * generation AST               effect AST
 *       |                             |
 *       +-------------+---------------+
 *                     |
 *                     v
 *              semantic analysis
 *                     |
 *                     v
 *              generation semantics
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 20. METAPROGRAMMING INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * `grammar/metaprogramming/generation.g4` owns:
 *
 *     generation
 *     generationRequest
 *     generationTargetedRequest
 *     generationConstruct
 *
 * `grammar/metaprogramming/metaprogramming.g4` owns metaprogramming
 * composition.
 *
 * This file must not redefine either subsystem.
 *
 * A generation construct may semantically imply or explicitly request a
 * code-generation effect.
 *
 * That association belongs to semantic analysis.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 21. MACRO INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Macro expansion remains owned by:
 *
 *     grammar/macros/
 *
 * A macro may generate source, but:
 *
 *     macro expansion
 *
 * and:
 *
 *     code-generation effect
 *
 * are not automatically equivalent.
 *
 * Semantic analysis determines whether a particular macro operation produces
 * or requires a code-generation effect.
 *
 * This grammar does not redefine macro syntax.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 22. REFLECTION INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Reflection remains owned by:
 *
 *     grammar/metaprogramming/reflection.g4
 *
 * Reflection may provide information consumed by code generation.
 *
 * Conversely, code generation may produce source that later undergoes
 * reflection.
 *
 * These are semantic relationships, not a reason to merge grammars.
 *
 * This file does not import or redefine reflection syntax.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 23. SPECIALIZATION INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Specialization remains owned by:
 *
 *     grammar/metaprogramming/specialization.g4
 *
 * Specialization may cause code generation as an implementation consequence.
 *
 * The semantic layer determines whether a specialization:
 *
 *     requires;
 *     produces;
 *     propagates;
 *     or discharges
 *
 * a code-generation effect.
 *
 * No specialization grammar is duplicated here.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 24. COMPILE-TIME EXECUTION INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Compile-time execution remains owned by the compile-time metaprogramming
 * subsystem.
 *
 * Compile-time execution MUST NOT be assumed to have unrestricted code
 * generation authority.
 *
 * A compile-time operation that generates source or artifacts may require
 * explicit semantic authorization.
 *
 * Parsing this effect does not authorize execution.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 25. QUOTATION / SYNTAX-TREE INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Quotation and syntax-tree construction remain owned by their respective
 * metaprogramming grammars.
 *
 * Generated syntax must preserve:
 *
 *     source span/origin information;
 *     transformation provenance;
 *     hygiene/scoping information where applicable;
 *     semantic context;
 *     compatibility information.
 *
 * Generated source must re-enter the normal Zamani frontend rather than
 * bypassing:
 *
 *     lexer
 *     parser
 *     AST
 *     validation
 *     semantic analysis
 *     effect analysis
 *     capability analysis
 *     resource analysis
 *     policy analysis
 *     provenance
 *     canonical IR lowering
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 26. EFFECT / CAPABILITY SEPARATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * A code-generation effect describes computational behavior.
 *
 * It does NOT automatically imply any capability.
 *
 * For example:
 *
 *     code_generation::generate
 *
 * does not itself imply:
 *
 *     filesystem.write
 *     process.execute
 *     native.execute
 *     network.access
 *     compiler.host.access
 *     device.access
 *
 * Those capabilities must be established separately by semantic analysis and
 * policy.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 27. EFFECT / RESOURCE SEPARATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * A code-generation effect does not encode:
 *
 *     memory capacity
 *     storage capacity
 *     processor count
 *     accelerator count
 *     execution width
 *     compilation time
 *     artifact count
 *     source size
 *     generated-code size
 *
 * Resource requirements are represented by the resource subsystem.
 *
 * This file does not allocate resources.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 28. EFFECT / POLICY SEPARATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Code generation may be governed by policies concerning:
 *
 *     source transformation
 *     generated artifacts
 *     target specialization
 *     native code
 *     foreign code
 *     filesystem access
 *     network access
 *     execution
 *     reproducibility
 *     provenance
 *     security
 *
 * Policy syntax remains owned by the policy/security subsystems.
 *
 * A syntactically valid code-generation effect is NOT authorization.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 29. EFFECT / CONTRACT SEPARATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Code-generation operations may participate in:
 *
 *     requires
 *     ensures
 *     invariant
 *     assume
 *     guarantee
 *     property
 *
 * Contract syntax remains owned by validation.
 *
 * This file does not duplicate contract syntax.
 *
 * Semantic analysis may associate contracts with a code-generation effect.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 30. PROVENANCE CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Code generation is transformation-heavy and therefore must integrate with
 * the canonical provenance model.
 *
 * Semantic provenance may record:
 *
 *     source identity
 *     source span
 *     generator identity
 *     transformation identity
 *     input identities
 *     generated artifact identity
 *     semantic version
 *     dialect/version
 *     policy decision
 *     capability decision
 *     resource realization
 *     verification result
 *     parent transformation
 *     generated-source origin
 *
 * This grammar does not create provenance records.
 *
 * It merely preserves the source structure required for semantic provenance.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 31. DETERMINISM CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Parsing this grammar MUST be deterministic.
 *
 * The parse result may depend only on:
 *
 *     source token stream
 *     grammar version
 *     canonical lexical configuration
 *     explicitly selected dialect configuration
 *
 * Parsing MUST NOT depend on:
 *
 *     compiler host
 *     filesystem state
 *     network state
 *     hardware availability
 *     device availability
 *     resource availability
 *     wall-clock time
 *     randomness
 *     runtime state
 *     backend selection
 *
 * Generation itself may be deterministic or nondeterministic depending on its
 * semantic contract.
 *
 * That distinction belongs downstream.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 32. REPRODUCIBILITY CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * When reproducibility is requested, semantic analysis may associate a
 * code-generation effect with:
 *
 *     source identity
 *     generator identity
 *     generator version
 *     dialect version
 *     semantic configuration
 *     input identity
 *     transformation sequence
 *     policy
 *     deterministic execution requirements
 *     provenance
 *
 * No fixed reproducibility implementation is mandated by this grammar.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 33. SECURITY CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Parsing a code-generation effect MUST NOT:
 *
 *     generate source
 *     write files
 *     execute generated programs
 *     invoke compilers
 *     invoke interpreters
 *     invoke shells
 *     access credentials
 *     access environment variables
 *     access devices
 *     access hardware
 *     access the network
 *     modify compiler state
 *     modify persistent state
 *
 * Such operations belong to downstream, explicitly authorized phases.
 *
 * Sandboxing and authorization remain semantic/security concerns.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 34. POCO-REAF CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Code-generation effects MUST preserve source portability.
 *
 * A source program must not become tied to the machine that compiled it merely
 * because code generation occurs.
 *
 * In particular, this grammar does not encode:
 *
 *     CPU identity
 *     GPU identity
 *     FPGA identity
 *     ASIC identity
 *     QPU identity
 *     physical qubit identity
 *     node identity
 *     device identity
 *     memory-bank identity
 *     physical address
 *     vendor identity
 *     topology
 *     bus width
 *     register width
 *
 * Target specialization belongs downstream.
 *
 * The semantic compiler may specialize generated representation for an
 * available target while preserving source-level program meaning and declared
 * constraints.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 35. QUANTUM BOUNDARY
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Code generation may generate or transform quantum source.
 *
 * This does NOT create a code-generation quantum IR.
 *
 * The required semantic path remains:
 *
 *     generated quantum source
 *          |
 *          v
 *     lexer
 *          |
 *          v
 *     parser
 *          |
 *          v
 *     domain-neutral AST
 *          |
 *          v
 *     quantum semantic analysis
 *          |
 *          v
 *     quantum::ir
 *          |
 *          v
 *     optimization
 *          |
 *          v
 *     decomposition
 *          |
 *          v
 *     routing
 *          |
 *          v
 *     scheduling
 *          |
 *          v
 *     resilience / QEC
 *          |
 *          v
 *     ZQN
 *          |
 *          v
 *     HAL
 *          |
 *          v
 *     target realization
 *
 * This file does not define:
 *
 *     qubits
 *     physical qubits
 *     quantum gates
 *     coupling maps
 *     calibration
 *     pulse schedules
 *     QEC codes
 *     decoders
 *     routing
 *     scheduling
 *     QPU identities
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 36. HDL / HARDWARE BOUNDARY
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Code generation may produce or transform:
 *
 *     HDL
 *     hardware intent
 *     accelerator descriptions
 *     configuration
 *     synthesis-related source
 *
 * Such syntax remains owned by:
 *
 *     grammar/hdl/
 *     grammar/hardware/
 *
 * This effect grammar does not define HDL syntax or hardware topology.
 *
 * Physical realization remains downstream.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 37. CLASSICAL / ACCELERATOR BOUNDARY
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Generated representations may eventually target:
 *
 *     classical computation
 *     tensor computation
 *     accelerator computation
 *     distributed computation
 *     networking
 *     embedded systems
 *     future computational substrates
 *
 * The effect grammar does not select any realization.
 *
 * Capability and resource analysis determine feasibility.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 38. DISTRIBUTED BOUNDARY
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Generated source may participate in:
 *
 *     parallel execution
 *     distributed execution
 *     actor systems
 *     service execution
 *     accelerator execution
 *     remote compilation
 *
 * This file does not define:
 *
 *     nodes
 *     worker counts
 *     cluster topology
 *     network topology
 *     deployment addresses
 *
 * Distributed realization remains downstream.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 39. FFI / ABI BOUNDARY
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Generated code may eventually cross:
 *
 *     FFI
 *     ABI
 *     foreign language
 *     native execution
 *
 * Those boundaries remain owned by:
 *
 *     grammar/interoperability/
 *     grammar/effects/foreign.g4
 *     grammar/effects/native.g4
 *
 * The semantic layer combines:
 *
 *     code-generation effect
 *     FFI requirements
 *     ABI requirements
 *     capability requirements
 *     security policy
 *     provenance
 *
 * This file does not duplicate FFI or ABI syntax.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 40. AI / KNOWLEDGE / REASONING INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Code generation may consume semantic results from:
 *
 *     reasoning
 *     inference
 *     learning
 *     adaptation
 *     knowledge
 *     uncertainty
 *     evidence
 *     provenance
 *
 * Such features remain owned by their respective grammar/semantic systems.
 *
 * For example, a compiler may semantically:
 *
 *     reason
 *         ->
 *     choose transformation
 *         ->
 *     generate representation
 *
 * This does not make reasoning syntax part of this effect grammar.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 41. ADAPTIVE GENERATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Generated code may be specialized according to:
 *
 *     capabilities
 *     resources
 *     policies
 *     contracts
 *     semantic constraints
 *     target-independent preferences
 *
 * However:
 *
 *     source intent
 *
 * must remain distinct from:
 *
 *     realization choice.
 *
 * The grammar does not inspect hardware to make this decision.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 42. SEMANTIC ADAPTATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * The code-generation effect must not be confused with unrestricted
 * self-modifying execution.
 *
 * The semantic model should distinguish:
 *
 *     compile-time transformation
 *
 * from:
 *
 *     runtime self-modification
 *
 * and:
